<main><article id="company-brain" tabindex="-1">
<header class="product-masthead page-width"><p class="eyebrow">CyberAlchemy Lab / Why we are building it</p><h1>A company brain<br>for work with AI.</h1><p class="article-deck">Keep the meaning, evidence and decisions behind work available to the next person—and the next agent.</p><p class="article-meta">{{READING_TIME}} minute read</p><a class="text-link" href="start-here.html">Explore the documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></header>
<div class="article-layout page-width">
<nav class="article-index" aria-label="In this article"><p class="eyebrow">In this article</p><a href="#continuity">The problem of continuity</a><a href="#company-knowledge">A company brain</a><a href="#governed-work">Knowledge that can guide work</a><a href="#incident">An incident, examined</a><a href="#agent-memory">What the next agent inherits</a><a href="#alignment">The alignment question</a><a href="#begin">Where to begin</a></nav>
<div class="article-body">
<section id="continuity" aria-labelledby="continuity-title"><h2 id="continuity-title">The work continues.<br>The context has to continue too.</h2>
<p>A customer reports a problem. Someone investigates it, reads the logs, forms a hypothesis and proposes a change. A colleague returns the next morning. Before doing useful work, they need to recover what happened: which data was examined, what “fixed” would mean, why a change was proposed and who can approve it.</p>
<p>Add AI agents to that process and the question becomes more pressing. A session can produce code, an assessment or a plan. The next session still needs a reliable account of what that work means. A convincing explanation gives a reviewer something to read; a connected record gives them something to examine.</p>
<p>CyberAlchemy Lab is being built for teams whose work depends on that continuity: software teams, research groups and operations teams coordinating people and agents across projects.</p>
</section>
<section id="company-knowledge" aria-labelledby="company-knowledge-title"><h2 id="company-knowledge-title">Give the company’s knowledge<br>a structure it can use.</h2>
<p>We call CyberAlchemy Lab an <strong>open-source company brain system</strong>. The name describes a practical ambition: keep knowledge connected to the work it informs, with enough structure for people and machines to inspect it.</p>
<p>That knowledge includes the meaning of important terms, the intentions behind a project, the evidence collected during work and the decisions that change what is accepted. The system organizes these as explicit records and declared connections.</p>
<p>A document store can preserve an explanation. The Lab model asks further questions about its role: does it define a meaning, record an observation, propose a change or authorize an effect? Each has different requirements. Making those roles explicit helps the next reader know how the information may be used.</p>
</section>
<section id="governed-work" aria-labelledby="governed-work-title"><h2 id="governed-work-title">Knowledge that can guide work.</h2>
<p>The Lab begins with definitions. When a project depends on “complete,” “valid” or “recovered,” those words need boundaries. An accepted definition gives an exact meaning authority within a declared scope. The associated record format expresses how that meaning is represented.</p>
<dl class="article-lenses"><div><dt>Authority</dt><dd>Which accepted meanings and rules apply, and who may act within which scope?</dd></div><div><dt>Provenance</dt><dd>Where did this information come from, and how was it produced?</dd></div><div><dt>Lifecycle</dt><dd>What state is this record in, and what conditions allow it to change?</dd></div></dl>
<p>Together, these questions let a team examine a claim and the proposed response. Evidence supports an assessment; an accountable decision determines the permitted change. A proposed fix remains a proposal until it follows its applicable review and authority route.</p>
<p>The intent explains what the team wants to accomplish. A product describes the continuing purpose of a system. A project coordinates work toward a bounded change. Work records describe the attempts made along the way. These distinctions keep the purpose visible as individual tasks move forward.</p>
</section>
<section id="incident" aria-labelledby="incident-title"><h2 id="incident-title">An incident, examined.</h2>
<p>Consider the fictional Northstar incident in the guide. A customer says its data export is missing records. An agent receives a bounded investigation assignment: inspect the saved source snapshot, output and logs; compare identifiers; record candidate assessment material; and prepare a proposal.</p>
<figure class="incident-strip"><div><small>Declared input</small><strong>{{REQUIRED_COUNT}}</strong><span>required identifiers</span><p>Account {{ACCOUNT}},<br>{{FILTERS}}, fixed snapshot {{SNAPSHOT}}.</p></div><div><small>Saved output</small><strong>{{OUTPUT_COUNT}}</strong><span>identifiers present</span><p>Inspect the saved export<br>{{OUTPUT}}, from run {{RUN}}.</p></div><div><small>Observed difference</small><strong>{{MISSING_COUNT}}</strong><span>required identifiers missing</span><p>Enough to establish an omission<br>in this output.</p></div><figcaption>A fictional comparison, used to explain the model. The source and output identities stay attached to the assessment.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The definition of completeness fixes the account, filters and source snapshot. The comparison can therefore establish a specific omission. The cause, wider impact and recovery still need investigation.</p>
<p>The assignment also makes the agent’s boundary clear. Preparing a change proposal falls within the investigation. Changing source data, deploying a fix, accepting a definition or closing the incident requires the relevant separate authority.</p>
<p>The result is an inspectable chain: an explicit criterion, identified inputs, a recorded comparison and a scoped proposal. The next reviewer can examine each part. <a href="incident-walkthrough.html">Follow the full incident walkthrough</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="agent-memory" aria-labelledby="agent-memory-title"><h2 id="agent-memory-title">What the next agent inherits.</h2>
<p>Machine agents are temporary. Their short-term memory belongs to the session. Continuing knowledge belongs to the project state: the durable records of meaning, work, evidence and decisions.</p>
<figure class="session-handoff"><div><small>Session A</small><strong>Investigate &amp; record</strong><span>Short-term session memory</span></div><div class="handoff-state"><small>Project state</small><strong>Continuing knowledge</strong><span>Recorded work, evidence &amp; decisions</span></div><div><small>Session B</small><strong>Recover &amp; continue</strong><span>Fresh context, checked permissions</span></div><figcaption>A session handoff. Records persist; a new session obtains the relevant context rather than inheriting an agent’s private memory.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Each new session receives a context view suited to its task. An ontology view shows the relevant concepts and their declared connections. Both come from project state and have conditions for use. If a view expires, the agent must refresh it or pause. Continuing the work requires usable context and applicable permission.</p>
<p>This is also where learning becomes a research question. A saved lesson can be examined and tried in later work. Claims of improvement need evidence of its actual use and an assessment of the observed benefit.</p>
</section>
<section id="alignment" aria-labelledby="alignment-title"><h2 id="alignment-title">A practical setting<br>for the alignment question.</h2>
<p>AI alignment concerns whether machine behavior follows human intentions and values. These are difficult to express completely, and people can disagree about them. The research spans how systems are trained, how their behavior is evaluated and how they can be controlled. <a class="source-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19852">Ji et al., <cite>AI Alignment: A Comprehensive Survey</cite></a>.</p>
<p>The Lab gives us a place to investigate part of that problem. Can clearer meanings reduce misunderstandings? Can scoped permissions and visible evidence help people detect a bad action sooner? Can project state preserve corrections across changing agents?</p>
<p>Those are research questions we can formulate and test. Governance records make intentions and decisions inspectable. Establishing how an agent actually behaves calls for evaluation under changing conditions, adversarial exercises and continuing oversight. <a class="source-link" href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/team/alignment">Anthropic’s alignment research</a> describes those complementary lines of inquiry.</p>
<p>Our ambition toward more general intelligence belongs beside this work. As a machine’s capabilities expand, our ability to express direction, examine behavior and revise the course must develop with them.</p>
</section>
<section id="begin" aria-labelledby="begin-title"><h2 id="begin-title">Begin with one piece of work.</h2>
<p>Take a project where people routinely reconstruct context. Ask what its important words mean, which evidence supports its current claims and who may make the next change. Those questions offer a concrete starting point for understanding the Lab.</p>
<p>The documentation lets you examine the model through a guided incident, a visual system map and the complete reference library. If your question concerns software behavior, Craft shows how explicit models can express it within the wider Lab.</p>
<div class="article-next"><a class="ds-button" href="start-here.html">Start with the Lab <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="text-link" href="governance-lab-reference.html#system-map">Explore the system map <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
</section>
</div></div></article></main>
